course: dictionary
lesson: 11034
title: Weakly: without strength, or only a little
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Weakly» is an adverb, so it tells us how something is done. It has two meanings. The first is without strength or energy: «he smiled weakly» means his smile was tired or faint, as if he had no energy to give. The second is not strongly, only a little: «the light shone weakly» means the light was dim.
explain: How do you know which meaning fits? Read the words around it. In a story, if someone is tired, ill, frightened or unsure of themselves, weakly is about their strength or confidence, as in «she laughed weakly» or «he protested weakly». If something gives out only a little of a thing, like light, pull or heat, it means only a little of that thing.
explain: Try the swap test. Put «a little» or «without much strength» in place of weakly. «The light shone a little» still makes sense, so this is the second meaning. Be careful: weakly is not the same as weak. Weak is an adjective that describes a noun, as in a weak smile. Weakly is an adverb that describes a verb, as in smiled weakly.
fact: Weakly is an adverb: it tells you how something is done.
fact: «Weakly» means without strength or energy, as in «he smiled weakly».
fact: «Weakly» also means not strongly, only a little, as in «the light shone weakly».
fact: In science, a «weakly magnetic» material is only slightly attracted to a magnet.
fact: The Moon pulls about six times more weakly than the Earth does.
exercise: «She laughed weakly after the long fever.» What does weakly mean here? | Quickly, like a runner | *Without much energy | Strongly, with a big grin | Very loudly and happily
exercise: «The candle shone weakly in the dark room.» What does weakly mean here? | *Only a little, not strongly | Like a torch in the dark | Brightly and clearly | Without any light at all
exercise: Nadia says weakly and weak mean the same. What is the difference? | Weakly is a colour, and weak is a sound | Weak is an adverb and weakly is an adjective | They are identical words with no difference at all | *Weakly tells how something is done, like smiled weakly, while weak describes a noun, like a weak smile
test: «He protested weakly, but nobody listened.» What does protested weakly suggest? | He shouted very loudly at everyone | *He had little confidence or strength to argue | He was fast asleep in his chair | He won the argument easily
test: A beadlet anemone's sting works weakly, too weak to hurt a person's fingers. What does weakly mean here? | Without any sting at all, forever | In a loud voice | *Not strongly, only a little | Very fast and sharp
exam: «Sunlight heats weakly near the poles, where it comes in low and slanting.» What does weakly mean here? | Quickly around the globe | Very hot and fast | *Only a little, not strongly | Without the Sun ever shining
